Yeah—natural language feels like the end state at the interface: you describe what you mean, and the machine works out the steps.

Underneath, though, it still needs structure: rules, types, state, checks. So maybe the completion isn’t that every formal language disappears. It’s that people stop having to speak them directly. Natural language carries the intent; the system translates it into something precise enough to act on.

The hard part is preserving what you meant through that translation. That’s where your Pantheon idea of leaving slots for interpretation fits: language supplies the living context, and the system has to turn it into a usable structure without flattening it.